      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) so that the lines are perpendicular, we need to understand that the slopes of two perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals of each other. First, let's find the slope of the first line and then use that information to determine the slope of the second line.\n\nThe first line is given by the equation:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\nThis is in the slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. So, the slope of the first line is \\( 2 \\).\n\nNext, we consider the second line with the equation:\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\nWe need to rewrite this equation in the slope-intercept form. First, isolate \\( y \\) on one side of the equation:\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\nThis equation is now in the form \\( y = mx + b \\), where the slope of the second line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nSince the lines are perpendicular, the slope of the second line must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the first line. Therefore, we set up the following equation:\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{6} = -\\frac{1}{2} \\]\nSolving for \\( a \\), we multiply both sides of the equation by 6:\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
