      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines with the given equations perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the slope of each line.\n2. Use the condition for perpendicular lines.\n3. Solve for \\(a\\).\n\nThe equations of the lines are:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\nFirst, rewrite the second equation in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\):\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{ax}{6} + 1 \\]\n\nSo, the slope of the second line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nNext, since the lines are perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\). The slope of the first line is \\(2\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{a}{3} = 1 \\]\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
