      "text": "1. We will start by rewriting the second equation in slope-intercept form.\n   \\begin{align*}\n   6y + ax &= 6 \\\\\n   y &= -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\end{align*}\n   The slope of the second line is $-\\frac{a}{6}$.\n\n2. The slopes of two perpendicular lines are opposite reciprocals of each other.\n   \\begin{align*}\n   m_1 \\cdot m_2 &= -1 \\\\\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) &= -1\n   \\end{align*}\n   This equation can be simplified as follows:\n   \\begin{align*}\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) &= -1 \\\\\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} &= -1 \\\\\n   \\frac{a}{3} &= 1 \\\\\n   a &= 3\n   \\end{align*}\n\n3. Hence, the value of $a$ that makes the lines perpendicular is $a = \\boxed{3}$. The final answer is \\boxed{3}.",
